==========================
LIFECYCLE-AWARE COMPONENTS
(Jetpack Compose – Theory)
==========================

1) WHAT IS A LIFECYCLE-AWARE COMPONENT?

A lifecycle-aware component is a class that reacts to lifecycle events
(onStart, onStop, onDestroy, etc.) WITHOUT being tightly coupled
to an Activity, Fragment, or UI code.

Example:
- DefaultLifecycleObserver
- ViewModel
- LiveData

Its main goal is:
✔ Separation of concerns
✔ Prevent memory leaks
✔ Reusable lifecycle logic


------------------------------------------------

2) WHY NOT USE ACTIVITY onCreate(), onStart(), onResume() DIRECTLY?

Activity lifecycle methods:
- Are tied to ONE Activity
- Mix UI + logic together
- Become messy as app grows
- Are hard to reuse and test

If you put camera, sensors, analytics, media logic directly
inside Activity methods, Activities become very large
("God Activities").

Lifecycle observers solve this by moving lifecycle logic OUT
of the Activity.


------------------------------------------------

3) WHAT DOES DefaultLifecycleObserver SOLVE?

DefaultLifecycleObserver:
- Receives lifecycle callbacks
- Is independent of UI
- Can be attached to:
  - Activity
  - Fragment
  - Compose screen
  - Any LifecycleOwner

This allows:
✔ Clean architecture
✔ Reusable behavior
✔ Same logic across multiple screens


------------------------------------------------

4) WHY IS THIS IMPORTANT IN JETPACK COMPOSE?

Jetpack Compose has NO:
- onCreate()
- onStart()
- onResume()

Composable functions:
- Can run multiple times (recomposition)
- Are NOT lifecycle callbacks
- Should contain ONLY UI logic

Because of this:
✔ Lifecycle logic CANNOT live inside composables
✔ We need a bridge to lifecycle

That bridge is:
LocalLifecycleOwner + DefaultLifecycleObserver


------------------------------------------------

5) ROLE OF DisposableEffect IN COMPOSE

DisposableEffect is used to run SIDE EFFECTS that:
- Touch the outside world
- Need cleanup

Examples:
- Lifecycle observers
- Sensors
- Camera
- Media
- Analytics

DisposableEffect guarantees:
✔ Observer added only once
✔ Observer removed automatically
✔ Safe during recomposition
✔ No memory leaks

Without DisposableEffect:
- Observer would be added multiple times
- Logs would repeat
- Memory leaks could occur


------------------------------------------------

6) WHY LIFECYCLE LOGIC IS KEPT IN A SEPARATE CLASS

Keeping lifecycle logic in a separate observer class:
✔ Follows Single Responsibility Principle
✔ Keeps UI clean
✔ Makes logic reusable
✔ Easier to test
✔ Easier to maintain

UI = UI only
Observer = lifecycle behavior only


------------------------------------------------

7) WHEN YOU DO NOT NEED DefaultLifecycleObserver

You do NOT need it when:
✔ Logic is already lifecycle-aware
✔ You are inside ViewModel
✔ You are handling simple UI state
✔ No external resources involved

Example:
- ViewModel (already lifecycle-aware)
- Business logic
- Repository logic


------------------------------------------------

8) WHEN YOU SHOULD USE DefaultLifecycleObserver

You SHOULD use it for:
✔ Camera start/stop
✔ Sensors register/unregister
✔ Media play/pause
✔ Location updates
✔ Analytics tracking
✔ Long-running listeners


------------------------------------------------

9) FINAL MENTAL MODEL

Compose UI
   ↓
LocalLifecycleOwner
   ↓
DisposableEffect
   ↓
DefaultLifecycleObserver
   ↓
Lifecycle-aware behavior


------------------------------------------------

10) INTERVIEW ONE-LINER

"In Jetpack Compose, DefaultLifecycleObserver combined with DisposableEffect
allows lifecycle-aware logic to be safely separated from UI, since composables
do not have lifecycle callbacks."

